 (:adr/related "adr-2609092400-the-checkout-was-the-stale-plane-and-a-program-outgrows-a-document, adr-2609092300-a-bounded-pattern-matcher-whose-program-is-data")
 (:adr/body "Continuing 2026-09-09: the named next step from ADR-2609092400 was to carry a
program as a `:string`, because a `:document` holds 32 items per container and
43 of the 200 regex literals in kotoba-lang's browser stack compile to more
than that.

## The string form, and the ceiling behind the ceiling

`kotoba-lang/pattern` gains `kotoba/pattern_vm.kotoba`: the same Pike VM over
a program carried as a string.

    header  \"K\" fold(1) nc(6 hex) nr(6 hex)      14 chars
    code    nc x [ op(1) a(6) b(6) c(1) ]        14 chars each
    ranges  nr x [ lo(6) hi(6) ]                 12 chars each

65536 / 14 is about 4,680 instructions against 32. There was nowhere else to
go: every guest container is bounded near 32 (typed map 31, typed set 32,
record 32 fields, heterogeneous vector 32), `[:vector T]` is a bounded TUPLE
type, and no backend has a sequence parameter type.

Measured on the real corpus: the IPv4 (88 instructions), the CSS length (82)
and the 64-hex id (65) now run. As documents they could not be handed to the
matcher at all.

LIFTING ONE CEILING LEFT THE NEXT ONE STANDING BEHIND IT. The thread list was
a document too, and 32 simultaneously-live threads is real: the
257-instruction email pattern from `kiyaku` answered `doc-vector-too-large` on
\"a@b.co\". The thread set is now one string as well -- a bitset of visited
program counters followed by the live list.

The bitset is not a matter of taste. With membership as a linear scan the same
pattern took 20.4 SECONDS for one `match?` -- correct, but the dedup that
keeps a Pike VM linear was itself quadratic in FUNCTION ENTRIES, and fuel is
charged per entry. With the bit test it is 3.5s, and the 88- and
65-instruction patterns are unchanged at 80 and 127 ms.

## The bug the corpus was hiding

`^(px|em)$` answered FALSE for \"px\". So did `^[-+]?[0-9]+$` for \"12\". `^a$`
for \"a\" was right, which is exactly why it survived: the anchor is reached
before any step happens.

The `eol` flag handed to a step was the one for the position the threads LEFT
rather than the one they land on, so every thread that stepped onto a `$` was
dropped. Both machines had it -- the document one had shipped with it.

216 parity checks agreed with JavaScript's own RegExp without touching it,
because THE CORPUS CONTAINED NO PATTERN ENDING IN `$`. This is the workspace's
own rule (\"that check has to have produced both answers\") failing in the one
way prose cannot catch: the corpus was the thing that was incomplete, not the
assertion. Four such patterns are in it now, and both suites are green at 252
(document) and 270 (string) checks.

## Three backend facts the string form cost

  * `bit-and` and `bit-or` are REFUSED by the JS backend -- \"unsupported KIR
    operation\" at the :kotoba-script phase -- while `string-from-i64` compiles
    there. The bit test is arithmetic instead: for a hex digit d and a bit
    value v, the bit is set exactly when (quot d v) is odd.
  * `i64-shift-left` takes a LITERAL count in [0,63], so a computed shift is a
    four-way branch.
  * `mod` is not a builtin here.

## What is not done

The COMPILER still builds its AST and program as documents, so it still cannot
emit the 43. The string emitter is the next slice, and it needs the AST to go
too -- the way out is to fuse parse and emit and re-emit an atom's source span
for a quantifier, rather than building a tree at all.

`compile_parity` now finds its corpus from a worktree as well; it had REFUSED
with \"0 patterns\", which is the guard working, but the path assumption was
wrong.
")))
